{"results":[{"url":"https://www.pennlive.com/news/2026/10/pennsylvania-pumpkins-picked-for-presidents-white-house-halloween-party.html","final_url":"https://www.pennlive.com/news/2026/10/pennsylvania-pumpkins-picked-for-presidents-white-house-halloween-party.html","title":"Pennsylvania pumpkins picked for president’s White House Halloween party","description":"Hundreds of pumpkins purchased at a Pa. produce auction were among the decorations at the White House for the annual Halloween party held on Thursday.","language":"en","text":"# Pennsylvania pumpkins picked for president’s White House Halloween party\n\n* Updated: Oct. 30, 2026, 10:48 p.m.\n* Published: Oct. 30, 2026, 10:12 p.m.\n\nThe South Portico of the White House is decorated before President Donald Trump and first lady Melania Trump arrive to greet families and hand out candy during a Halloween event on the South Lawn of the White House, Thursday, Oct. 29, 2026, in Washington. (AP Photo/Mark Schiefelbein)AP Photo/Mark Schiefelbein\n\nBy \n\n* John Beauge | Special to PennLive\n\nMIFFLINBURG — Hundreds of pumpkins purchased at a Union County produce auction were among the decorations at the White House for the annual Halloween party held on Thursday.\n\nIt was the third year in a row a customer from Virginia bought pumpkins at the Buffalo Valley Produce Auction some of which ended up at the White House, said general manager and auctioneer Neil Courtney.\n\nAsked for his reaction to having pumpkins selected for such a prestigious display, he said: “It tells you we have a great crop here. It’s a good feeling. It’s not about politics.”\n\nCourtney auctions pumpkins twice a week during September and October. One of his customers is Cary Nalls who operates Nalls Produce in Alexandria, Virginia.\n\nNalls explained that he helps meet the needs of the White House in conjunction with Frey Farms of Orchardville, Illinois.\n\nThe Frey people came to him because he was closer to Washington, Nalls said.\n\nThe pumpkins Nalls said he provided for the White House ranged in size from very small to some that weighed 1,000 pounds. Quality was very important, he said.\n\nThe annual Halloween event is a longstanding tradition at the White House, where children and their parents visit trick-or-treat booths on the South Lawn amid spooky decorations.\n\nPresident Donald Trump and First Lady Melania Trump were present Thursday to greet approximately 6,000 children of military, law enforcement, foster and adoptive families along with administration officials and their children.\n\nMost of the pumpkins Nalls bought at the auction likely were grown in Pennsylvania, Courtney said. Almost all of those auctioned at Buffalo Valley are grown within 100 miles of Mifflinburg, he said.\n\nIt was a good year for pumpkins, he said. The supply was high, the demand was okay and the prices were down a little, he said. “Everyone that had them had a full crop,” he said.\n\nWith pumpkin sales over, Courtney is getting ready for what is billed as the world’s largest wholesale Christmas tree auction that will be Nov. 20. Decorative items will be sold the day before.\n\nJohn Beauge\n\nWilliamsport native John Beauge obtained a bachelor’s degree in journalism from Penn State and a master’s from Northwestern University. He was photo editor of the Daily Collegian his senior year at Penn State....\n\nJohn.Beauge@outlook.com\n\n## Comments\n\nPlease take a moment to review our community guidelines. To change your display name, go to your account settings.","author":"John Beauge","published_date":"2026-10-31T02:12:38.537Z","latency_ms":22959.41288100039,"format":"markdown"}],"errors":[]}